# Can light be a feeling? Van Gogh's The Starry Night
tags: ["art history","van gogh","starry night","light in painting","emotion and color","impressionism"]
teaches: ["art-history/symbolism-in-painting","concepts/emotional-light","visual-analysis/composition","art-appreciation/expressionism"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, knowledgeable gallery guide, quietly amused","b":"gemini:Puck | a relaxed, good-humoured visitor with a dry, understated wit; curious, never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What is real in this sky, and what isn't? Where does the light come from now?"

## The Starry Night: light as feeling
sources:
    - id: wiki
      title: The Starry Night
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: June 1889, east view from his asylum room at Saint-Rémy; Venus visible at dawn, near its brightest; the Moon waning gibbous, painted as a crescent; the village imaginary, steeple read as Dutch; cypress enlarged; swirls read as a spiral nebula or the mistral; called a failure in a letter to Bernard, Nov 1889; MoMA since 1941
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Starry_Night
    - id: moma
      title: The Starry Night (1889)
      authors: The Museum of Modern Art, New York
      year: 2024
      finding: Saint-Rémy, June 1889; oil on canvas, 73.7 × 92.1 cm; acquired through the Lillie P. Bliss Bequest (by exchange), 1941
    - id: letter
      title: Letter 777 to Theo van Gogh, c. 31 May–6 June 1889
      authors: Vincent van Gogh (Van Gogh Museum edition)
      year: 1889
      finding: Saw the countryside from his window long before sunrise, with only the morning star, which looked very big
      url: https://vangoghletters.org/vg/letters/let777/letter.html

    card title "The Starry Night"

B: This one I know: Van Gogh's The Starry Night. It's on half the mugs in the world.
    image art cites ["wiki","moma","letter"] credit "Van Gogh, June 1889. Museum of Modern Art, New York. Public domain." x 500 y 340 width 820 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Van_Gogh_-_Starry_Night_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Van_Gogh_-_Starry_Night_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.crescent [0.84,0.1,0.13,0.17] regions.cypress [0.12,0.03,0.34,0.97] regions.cypress_body [0.15,0.3,0.12,0.4] regions.dawn [0.45,0.36,0.55,0.16] regions.hills [0.4,0.5,0.6,0.25] regions.moon [0.78,0.04,0.21,0.3] regions.sky [0,0,1,0.62] regions.star_halo [0.655,0.165,0.1,0.13] regions.stars [0.04,0,0.68,0.5] regions.steeple [0.53,0.62,0.07,0.26] regions.swirl [0.36,0.15,0.4,0.38] regions.venus [0.295,0.45,0.125,0.155] regions.village [0.43,0.68,0.57,0.32] regions.windows [0.47,0.74,0.45,0.15]

A: Painted in June 1889, and our last question: can light be a feeling?

A: Van Gogh was in an asylum near Saint-Rémy, in the south of France,
    portrait vangogh of "Vincent van Gogh" cameo true

    erase vangogh

A: and this is the view east from his bedroom window.
    highlight art:hills lift true

B: Is any of it real? That sky looks like it's boiling.
    point at.ref art:swirl gesture glow

A: More than you'd think. Early that June, he wrote to his brother Theo about the view before sunrise, with only the morning star, which looked very big.
    highlight art:dawn strength soft lift true

    ask "Which one is the morning star? Tap it." spot venus on art voice a
        right: Yes: Venus. Astronomers have checked: that spring it shone at dawn in the east, almost as bright as it ever gets.
        wrong: Look for the biggest, whitest star, close to the tall dark tree.

    camera on ["art@[0.2,0.36,0.32,0.34]"]

B: So the star is real.
    highlight art:venus strength strong rest grey lift true

    camera on ["art:moon"]

B: What about the moon?
    highlight art:crescent lift true

A: Real, but not this one: that week the moon was more than half full, not a thin crescent.
    highlight art:crescent

    camera on ["art:village"]

B: And the village looks peaceful enough.
    highlight art:village lift true

A: Invented. No village could be seen from his window; he worked it up from a sketch,
    highlight art:windows

A: and some read the st (@highlight art:steeple@)eeple as a memory of Dutch churches from home.

    camera reset true

B: The cypress, then. Trees don't move.
    point at.ref art:cypress_body gesture glow

A: He pulled the cypress close and made it huge, tall enough to reach the stars.
    highlight art:cypress lift true

B: That leaves the sky. Nobody has ever seen a sky do that.
    highlight art:swirl lift true

    camera on ["art:swirl"]

A: Nobody has. Some see a spiral galaxy from the astronomy books of his day, others the mistral wind; the painting doesn't say.
    highlight art:swirl

    camera reset true

B: So where is the light coming from? There's no beam, no candle, no sun.
    highlight art:sky strength soft lift true

    quiz "Where does the light come from? Pick one." on_canvas true buttons_at.x 330 buttons_at.y 470 voice a
        right: Everywhere at once: the stars, the moon and the windows each shine by themselves, and no one source rules the sky.
        wrong: Look for shadows. One source would throw them.
        * The moon
        + Everywhere at once
        * The stars, all equally
        * The village windows

    camera on ["art:star_halo"] zoom 3

A: Every light carries its own halo, ring after ring of strokes, and nothing casts a shadow.
    highlight art:star_halo lift true

    camera reset true

A: The light doesn't fall on the world any more; it turns through it, and the whole sky moves with the brush.
    highlight art:sky strength spotlight rest blur lift true

B: So it's less what the sky looked like, and more what it felt like to stand under it.
    highlight art:sky lift true

A: Yes. Some see turmoil in it, some see hope; either way, the light now carries a feeling, not just a view.
    highlight art:sky strength soft lift true

    quiz "A photographer wants a night photo of a city that feels the way this sky does. Which follows Van Gogh?" voice a
        right: The swirled lamps: they are the city's real lights, but moved by how it felt to be there.
        wrong: Keep what was really there, and show how it felt.
        * A sharp, still long exposure
        * Invented lights in a real street
        + The real lamps, swirled
        * One streetlamp on one face

B: He must have known he'd made something special.

A: He didn't. A few months later he called it a failure. It has hung in New York's Museum of Modern Art since 1941.

A: Van Eyck's glazes and Caravaggio's beam made light fall on things (@highlight art:sky lift true@). Here, the light is the feeling itself.

B: Ten paintings on, and the light has moved from the window into the painter.
    highlight art:stars strength soft lift true

## Where next
